\chapter{Conditions and loops}
\api{if} evaluates a condition and executes one braced branch. An optional
\api{else} supplies the false branch, and \api{else if} chains conditions.
Parentheses around the condition are optional because braces delimit the body.

\begin{mullcode}
var total <- 0;
var i <- 0;
while i < 10 {
    i <- i + 1;
    if i == 6 { break; }
    if i % 2 == 0 { total <- total + i; }
    else { total <- total + 1; }
}
G:Assert(total == 9);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Loop scope}
\api{while} reevaluates its condition before every iteration. Each execution
of the body creates a fresh lexical scope. Declare a running counter outside
the loop when it must survive across iterations. A closure created within
an iteration retains that iteration's bindings.

\api{break;} exits the nearest enclosing loop. It cannot cross a function
boundary: calling a function containing an otherwise unhandled break raises
an error even if the caller happens to be inside a loop. \api{return} exits
the current function, or ends a top-level run or package evaluation.

The interpreter counts evaluation steps and allocated environments. An
unbounded loop eventually raises a limit error under the default settings.
Limits are configurable by the host, and a subsequent \api{run} receives a
fresh step budget. Previously completed assignments remain visible after
an interrupted loop.
